WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:06.750
At the end of the first millennium, around
1000 AD, China was definitively the most powerful

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.730
country in the world.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.140
More than a third of the world lived within
its borders, it’s technology was the most

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:18.980
advanced in existence, and its economy accounted
for an astronomical 50% of the worlds GDP.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.220
The west paled in comparison to China, but
eventually, Europe arose from its dark ages,

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:28.840
the importance of China diminished, and the
west came to rule the world.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.780
Today that is still largely the case, but
China is rising again.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:40.969
In 1978 China had a GDP of only $200 billion,
only about 4% of the world’s GDP, but nowadays

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:49.209
that GDP has risen to $11 trillion and accounts
for 15% of all economic activity in the world.

00:00:49.209 --> 00:00:54.829
This economic renaissance of the last 40 years
is largely thanks to one industry—manufacturing.

00:00:54.829 --> 00:01:01.209
We’ve come to accept that China is the world’s
factory, but it wasn’t always this way.

00:01:01.209 --> 00:01:05.059
In the early 20th century goods were often
just produced right near where they were sold.

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:07.729
America made American goods, Europe made European
goods.

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:11.479
It wasn’t until cheap, worldwide shipping
became available that the production side

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:15.890
of a company could be relocated to the other
side of the world, but why did China win?

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.700
How did this country become the manufacturing
giant it is today?

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:24.049
In 1978, Deng Xiaoping took power in the People’s
Republic of China.

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:29.119
He quickly visited Bangkok, Singapore, and
other flourishing Asian cities and was convinced

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:33.469
that, in order to succeed, China needed to
open itself up to the outside world, at least

00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:34.469
to an extent.

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:39.210
He gave people control of their farms, privatized
businesses, and, most importantly, allowed

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:42.549
foreign investment in the country for the
first time in decades.

00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:47.229
He opened up four special economic zones with
tax incentives and exemption from the oversight

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:50.999
that the rest of the country saw on its investments
and trade activity.

00:01:50.999 --> 00:01:55.649
These four zones were essentially the free
market portions of China, but none was more

00:01:55.649 --> 00:02:00.520
successful than this one—Shenzhen in the
Guangdong Province so I went there to see

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.079
what it was like.

00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:07.670
Before its designation as a special economic
zone in 1980, Shenzhen was a tiny town with

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:13.280
about 30,000 inhabitants but today that’s
grown to nearly 18 million people.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.810
That means its size rivals that of New York
and London.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:23.040
It’s believed that Shenzhen might have been
the single fastest growing city in human history.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.760
Every other Special Economic Zone was an established
area before its designation, but Shenzhen

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.269
made sense as a spot where China could embrace
the west as it lay just north of the border

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:35.939
of Hong Kong—what was at the time a British
territory.

00:02:35.939 --> 00:02:39.989
Today Shenzhen is the electronics manufacturing
capital of the world.

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:44.000
“Shenzhen as a city is really known for
two things.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.879
One is manufacturing capabilities especially
for consumer electronics products and second,

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:58.730
its gravitation for talents or human resources
especially on the product development or research

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.290
and development disciplines.”

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:06.640
Apple, Samsung, Microsoft, Sony, Canon—they
all manufacture products here in Shenzhen.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.780
In fact, 90% of the world’s electronics
are made at least in part in this city.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.950
Everything just costs less in China so labor
costs less too.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:21.269
Where a factory in the United States might
pay upwards of $10-15 an hour, a Chinese laborer

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:25.140
would happily accept $3 or $4 an hour in Shenzhen.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.900
When I was there I withdrew the equivalent
of $80 from the ATM upon arrival and left

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.819
four days later with cash leftover.

00:03:31.819 --> 00:03:35.250
A 30 minute taxi ride cost about $7 US dollars.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.180
A full meal at a local place was about $3.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.500
But China isn’t cheap entirely naturally.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.750
China has artificially depressed the value
of their currency.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:49.900
Up until 2005, the Chinese government just
said that the exchange rate was 8.27 yuan

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.530
per dollar and that was that.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.989
They then went a few years allowing it to
increase in value within a margin, but in

00:03:55.989 --> 00:04:00.390
2008 they pegged its value again to make Chinese
exports more attractive during the financial

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:01.390
crisis.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:05.219
Nowadays, the government just picks a exchange
rate daily and lets the currency fluctuate

00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:07.390
from it by up to 2%.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.920
This just makes it so western companies can
buy more for less.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:16.120
China also doesn’t charge taxes on exports
while the US doesn’t charge taxes on imports.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.700
The US doesn’t even charge customs fees
for some products like tablet PC’s so some

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.840
products can make it all the way from their
factory in China to stores in the US completely

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.010
tax free.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.920
When I was in Shenzhen I visited a company
called Anker.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.580
They make all sorts of consumer electronics
but chances are if you have something from

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.210
them, it’s one of their portable batteries.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:39.560
I asked their CEO why so many electronics
companies are now based in Shenzhen.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:43.510
“So the advantage is that, you know, we’re
close to the supply chain so everything is

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:44.510
faster.

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:52.820
Right, so, instead of like, you know, you
do a mock up and you see it two weeks later

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:58.070
here you send design to mockup and you receive
it, like, three days later.”

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:02.160
In Shenzhen there are markets where you can
buy every part imaginable, there are factories

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.811
ready to build prototypes in a matter of days,
there are engineers ready to work at the drop

00:05:06.811 --> 00:05:08.340
of a hat.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.140
Development just happens faster in Shenzhen.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:14.830
A US company might use the same factories
as one based in Shenzhen but the geographical

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:16.770
distance makes production slower.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.550
In addition, word travels fast in Shenzhen.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:24.030
A few years ago Anker was first-to-market
with a technology called PowerIQ that allows

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.460
for faster device charging.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:29.060
The engineers from Anker told me that a big
reason they were able to go to market before

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:33.030
the western companies was because they heard
about it first thanks to their proximity to

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:34.890
other engineers and companies.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:39.970
Shenzhen just produces things better and faster,
but a product isn’t just a physical item.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:47.860
“Honestly being in Shenzhen for this company
is an advantage for the supply chain that

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:57.260
we can develop good products with a competitive
price but for the brand its also a kind of

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:58.260
a challenge.”

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.790
What Shenzhen can’t build as well is brands.

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:05.250
Certain companies like Anker have been able
to to an extent thanks to smart PR and marketing,

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.840
but some other companies just don’t bother.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.240
Lucrative western consumers want familiar
and approachable feeling brands but cultural

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.730
differences and geographic distance often
make China based companies just seem different.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:22.021
All across Shenzhen I saw these boxes filled
with rentable portable chargers and as it

00:06:22.021 --> 00:06:26.690
turns out, it was an Anker product but the
Chinese designed product didn’t really succeed

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.610
in the western market.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:35.390
“Talking about AnkerBox, the product is
initially designed for the western market,

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:42.540
however, actually, our trial in Seattle wasn’t
that successful so we realized that, first

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:49.670
of all, that US people actually, when they
go to a bar they actually really go there

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.920
drinking instead of looking at their phones.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:58.650
Well if you walk into a bar in China you’ll
find that actually, like 20 tables of people

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.860
standing around looking at their phones.”

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:04.490
When the designers are thousands of miles
away from the consumer they might not be as

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.380
knowledgeable of their wants.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:10.440
Some companies have sprung up in Shenzhen
whose whole business is to develop and produce

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.280
products without a brand.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.160
They’re called “white label companies.”

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.160
They might produce earphones, for example,
then sell them to a western audio company

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.960
who will attach their brand and sell the product
at a mark-up.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:27.330
Despite it’s enormous role in increasing
the GDP of China 30-fold in the last 30 years,

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:30.430
manufacturing is not an entirely sustainable
industry for the country long-term.

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:35.490
The problem is that, rather ironically, the
economic growth that manufacturing spurred

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:40.710
in China has increased labor prices to a point
where their manufacturing is less competitive.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:45.890
Before manufacturing came in China was a country
of poor, rural farmers but today China has

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:49.330
a real middle class and cities that are expensive
to live in.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:53.390
It used to be that workers like the ones at
Anker’s factory moved to the city for a

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:57.300
few years when they were young to make money
for their family back home but nowadays people

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.760
are moving to cities permanently and want
to be able to set up solid, middle class,

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.000
urban lives.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:08.740
The average cost for real-estate in central
Shenzhen is almost $1,200 per square foot.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:11.690
That’s even higher than San Francisco and
New York.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:16.650
Even when workers live outside the city center,
higher wages are necessary even just to pay

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:17.650
for housing.

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:22.510
At the same time manufacturing is becoming
less labor intensive every day as robots and

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:26.920
automation are becoming increasingly advanced
and inexpensive.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:31.450
In 2015 China launched a initiative spending
hundreds of billions of dollars each year

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:35.579
to upgrade and automate factories in order
to keep prices low, but this will likely do

00:08:35.579 --> 00:08:39.729
little to keep companies from packing up shop
and moving elsewhere.

00:08:39.729 --> 00:08:43.110
Manufacturing lines that can be automated
are likely to move back to the United States

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.560
and the rest of the western market.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:49.150
Robots cost the same whether they’re in
the United States or China so manufacturing

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:53.620
products in the US helps save on shipping
costs and certainly is good for PR.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:58.270
At the same time, the labor intensive jobs
that China prospered on in past decades are

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:03.570
moving to less developed and less expensive
countries like Vietnam, Bangladesh, and India.

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:07.339
Chinese manufacturing firms are responding
to this by opening up their own factories

00:09:07.339 --> 00:09:11.550
all across the world—everywhere from Africa
to the United States itself.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:16.019
The model that might work for China in the
future is that of Anker—Chinese based firms

00:09:16.019 --> 00:09:20.329
that can take advantage of their proximity
to the production lines to cut down on development

00:09:20.329 --> 00:09:21.589
cost and time.

00:09:21.589 --> 00:09:26.899
Shenzhen based start-ups like Anker, DJI,
and OnePlus have already succeeded in taking

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:30.800
advantages of this proximity, but more are
being established each day.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:35.759
If you see a hardware-based Kickstarter campaign,
there’s a good chance it comes from Shenzhen.

00:09:35.759 --> 00:09:40.519
10 years ago a company would be hard-pressed
to succeed in Shenzhen as its own brand because

00:09:40.519 --> 00:09:45.959
historically the retailer has acted as a barrier
in between the manufacturer and consumer,

00:09:45.959 --> 00:09:51.329
but with the rise of Amazon and other e-commerce
sites its now possible for eastern companies

00:09:51.329 --> 00:09:56.230
to sell directly to the western consumer in
a system that rewards for quality over price.

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:59.600
The time really is ripe for Chinese entrepreneurship.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:04.220
While Silicon Valley might be the dominant
area for software start-ups, its hard to rival

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:08.850
Shenzhen as an ecosystem for hardware development
and manufacturing.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:13.430
I have to give a huge thanks to Anker for
bringing me out to Shenzhen to research and

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:14.720
film this video.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.660
It truly would not have been possible without
them and they gave me complete creative control

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:19.660
over the video.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:24.490
I’ve actually owned and used an Anker battery
for over five years thats never broken down

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:28.310
even when it’s been accidentally submerged
twice and dropped countless times but luckily

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:30.980
they upgraded me to one of their newest ones
which I love.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:34.360
The main thing I want to plug for them is
their “Power it Up” contest going on right

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:35.360
now.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.670
10 winners will each get $2,000 in cash and
a bunch of free Anker products.

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:43.110
To enter you make a video up to 1 minute long
about an unpleasant or awkward situation caused

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:47.430
by running out of power, upload it to youtube,
then share it on their page and the 10 highest

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:49.470
voted ones at the end of the contest win.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.370
The link to enter is in the description so
good luck!

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:56.180
Aside from that, please be sure to check out
my podcast Showmakers and subscribe to this

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:59.259
channel to get all my future videos right
when they come out.

00:10:59.259 --> 00:11:03.209
Thanks again for watching and I’ll see you
next week for another Wendover Productions

00:11:03.209 --> 00:11:03.350
video.

